format 1
adapter safety 1
section: basics

card safety-time-distance-shielding
title: Time, distance, shielding
icon: Timer1
entry: shielding
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself
summary: Three ways to lower the dose from a radiation source.
steps: Time: limiting the time you are exposed reduces the dose from a source.
steps: Distance: the dose decreases dramatically as you move farther from the source.
steps: Shielding: barriers of lead, concrete or water protect against penetrating gamma rays.

card safety-background-radiation
title: Background radiation
icon: Sun1
entry: background-radiation
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation
summary: Radiation has always been part of the natural environment.
steps: Background radiation is radiation that is always in the environment. Most of it is natural.
steps: Radon gas from rock and soil is the main natural source. Cosmic rays and minerals in the ground add more.
steps: Food and water carry some natural radioactive material, such as potassium-40.
steps: The most common human-made source of radiation exposure is medical imaging, such as x-rays and CT scans.

card safety-what-is-a-dose
title: What a dose is
icon: Chart
entry: absorbed-dose
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation
summary: How much radiation the body takes in, and how fast, both matter.
steps: Damage depends on the absorbed dose, measured in grays. The effective dose is in sieverts.
steps: The dose rate matters too: it is how fast the dose is delivered.
steps: Effects such as burns are more severe at higher doses and higher dose rates.
steps: Low doses delivered slowly carry a lower risk, because the body is more likely to repair the damage.

card safety-exposure-vs-contamination
title: Exposure or contamination?
icon: Drop
entry: exposure
references: ref-hps-radioactive-contamination
summary: Radioactive contamination can be on or inside a person. Radiation itself does not spread.
steps: Contamination is radioactive material deposited on skin, clothing or anywhere it is not wanted.
steps: External contamination: radioactive material on the skin or clothing.
steps: Internal contamination: it is breathed in, swallowed or absorbed through wounds.
steps: Radiation does not spread or get on or in people. Radioactive contamination is what can be spread.
steps: A contaminated person receives radiation exposure until the radioactive material is removed.
